sys_mmapper map/unmap/free memory: bookkeeping, unmap's mem_id out-parameter, alias/move warnings, a trace.

Symptom: Killzone 2 (1.29) runs its own page heap on sys_mmapper: it reserves 0x20000000..0x2FFFFFFF
(sys_mmapper_allocate_address at 0x1F3B8; a second 256 MB reservation with flags 0x208 at 0x72BD8) and maps and
unmaps 1 MB pages there (0x11FF4 / 0x11D74), and 0x72BD8 maps one 64 KB block at several addresses. In the flat VM
sys_mmapper_map_memory, unmap_memory and free_memory were plain CELL_OK stubs: unmap never wrote the mem_id it
returns, an id mapped at two addresses does not share bytes, and an id re-mapped elsewhere does not carry its
contents. Nothing recorded which of these the game actually does.
Fix: keep an addr -> mem_id table; unmap writes the id mapped at addr into *r4. Always (first 16 each): a WARNING
line when an id is mapped at a second address while still mapped (alias) or at a different address than the one it
was last unmapped from (move). Diagnostics, off by default: KZ2_MMAP_LOG=<n> logs the first n map/unmap/free calls
with the caller, and every sys_mmapper_allocate_address (lv2 330 in the loader); KZ2_MMAP_MOVE=1 saves a block's
bytes at unmap and copies them to the new address on a move, so contents follow the id as on the PS3.

Verification: Killzone 2 under Proton.
v0.17.0 rebase (ps3recomp a6d76c8): 6015 allocate_address log moved after #296's sys_mmapper_allocate_address (address read back from *out).

Rebased on ps3recomp v0.18.0: context and offsets only.

